  "introductionText": "1. What FRUS answers directly. The series, Foreign Relations of the United States (FRUS), answers both halves of the question, and mostly in the Department of State’s own instructions. From 1877 to 1949 the Department described its test for a government that had taken power by force in nearly the same words: does it hold the machinery of government, does the population accept it, and can and will it meet the state’s international obligations (FRUS 1877, Document 222; FRUS 1900, Document 530; FRUS 1948, The Western Hemisphere, Volume IX, Document 7). In two periods Washington demanded more than that: under Wilson it asked for constitutional legitimacy (FRUS 1913, Document 7), and from 1923 to 1936 it followed a Central American treaty that barred recognition of any government that took power by coup or revolution (FRUS 1931, Volume II, Document 232; FRUS Diplomatic Papers, 1936, The American Republics, Volume V, Document 109). What it asked of the new rulers before recognizing them was never one fixed price. It asked for assurances about treaties and the treatment of foreigners (Mexico, 1877; Chile, 1932), for a treaty (Haiti, 1915, giving the United States financial control and a right to intervene; Mexico, 1921, protecting American property), for proof of popular support (Cuba, 1934), for a date for elections or for civilian rule (the Dominican Republic and Ecuador, 1963), and for a clean record against the Axis or against communism (Bolivia, 1943–44 and 1964); from 1943 it also asked the other American republics. The raw material is large: about 2,900 of the 57,000 documents in the Latin American parts of the series, roughly one in twenty, mention recognition close to the word government, regime, junta or administration, and about three in five of those, judging from a sample of forty, are about recognizing or refusing to recognize a Latin American government. Forty-five documents carry the argument, and the collection that goes with this overview holds them.\n\n2. Where FRUS points for the rest. There are two channels, reported separately and never added together. Came from is the archive each printed document was taken from. Most of them — 30 of the 45 documents I rely on, and about two in three of the 2,900 candidates — carry file numbers from the Department of State’s central decimal file for 1910–1963, which is part of Record Group 59 (the Department’s records at the National Archives, NARA); the reference data shipped with the app gives this file no NARA identifier, entry number or access status, and I have not supplied them from memory. For 1963–73 the source is the Department’s subject-numeric central files (also Record Group 59), whose headings for independence and recognition (POL 16) and for rebellion and coups (POL 26, later POL 23-9) hold the recognition files; again no identifier is given. Two lots of the Department’s working files supply documents I rely on (a lot is a batch of an office’s files, cited by number): the Policy Planning Staff papers (lot 64 D 563, which NARA divides among twelve series, so the paper itself may sit in any of them; the staff studies of 1947–49, NARA 1151288, entry A1 558-A, dates 1947–1949, unrestricted, are the likeliest) and the Department’s National Security Council files (lot 62 D 1, which NARA divides among three series, so a paper may sit in any of them: Alphabetical Files, NARA 2198167, entries A1 1583A–B, 1948–1961, partly restricted; Numerical Files, NARA 2838991, entries A1 1583C–D, 1947–1961, access status not given, the likeliest home of a numbered policy paper; and Records Relating to Activities with the National Security Council, NARA 2838992, entries A1 1583E–F, 1948–1961, partly restricted). A smaller group of later documents comes from presidential libraries, whose collections the reference data resolves to the libraries’ own finding aids and, in the catalog it ships, to NARA identifiers and dates but not access status: the Kennedy Library’s National Security Files (NARA 1164; its Country Files, NARA 193236, 1961–1963), the Johnson Library’s National Security Files (NARA 567979; its Latin America Country Files, NARA 567985, 1963–1969) and the Nixon Library’s National Security Council Files (the catalog’s Institutional Files, NARA 572247, 1969–1974, are the likeliest match for the Institutional (H) Files cited); the Eisenhower Library’s Project Clean Up records are in neither the catalog nor the finding aids. The nineteenth-century documents print no source note (nearly a quarter of the candidates, almost all before 1910); the reference data places such instructions on NARA’s microfilmed volumes of Diplomatic Instructions (series 593313; Mexico, 1873–79, is roll 149324017, and Colombia, 1898–1906, roll 149302787) and Despatches (series 603720), which is an inference from country and date, not a citation. Pointed at is what the editors’ footnotes cite without printing, and it is thin: only about one candidate in fourteen carries such a footnote, naming central-file classes (about 150 documents, 1912–62), lot files of Department offices, most of them the Bureau of Inter-American Affairs’ (about 35, 1950–80) or presidential libraries (about 35, 1955–80). Among those lots are the Assistant Secretary’s subject files for 1957–59 (lots 59 D 573 and 61 D 279; NARA 2108779; entry A1 1135; dates 1957–1959; unrestricted), the Central American and Panamanian office’s subject and country files (lot 63 D 127; NARA 2363840; A1 3157; 1955–1963; restricted possibly), the El Salvador records (lot 64 D 16; NARA 1750062; A1 3159; 1958–1975; partly restricted) and the Ecuador subject files (lot 70 D 247; NARA 26309336; A1 5741; 1964–1978; partly restricted). Recorded footnote citations begin in December 1910, in the Latin American parts and in the whole series alike, so for the nineteenth century this channel is empty, not negative.\n\n3. What this request cannot get from FRUS. (a) The Department’s own reasoning before 1920. I searched the Latin American parts of all 553 volumes for recognition within six words of a government, regime, junta or administration. That finds about 1,150 documents dated 1861–1919, of which only six are internal memoranda, papers or minutes, against more than half (263 of 476) in 1950–79; the same test on document headings finds tens of thousands of memoranda across the series and nothing for a phrase that cannot occur. Before 1920 the printed record is the instruction going out and the despatch coming in; the reasoning is on the shelf, in the microfilmed Diplomatic Instructions and Despatches, in the Department’s Numerical File on microfilm (series NARA 654171, which the data indexes by case number, not by date) and, from 1910, in the central decimal file. (b) The labels historians use. “Wilson Doctrine” and “Mann Doctrine” appear in no document of the series, “Tobar Doctrine” in one, “Betancourt Doctrine” in three and “Estrada Doctrine” in 25 of the Latin American documents (the phrase reads literally the same way in 24 of the 25; the other puts Estrada in quotation marks). Officials described their own rule as the traditional policy, so a search for doctrine names finds the Latin American rules (Estrada’s, Betancourt’s, Tobar’s), not the American one. (c) The new governments’ own reasoning. Spanish recognition vocabulary appears in only eleven of the 57,000 documents, so what the new governments said reaches the series almost entirely in English, in American reports and translations; their foreign ministries’ files are the shelf, and none of the archival reference files shipped with the app lists them (a search of that data for the ministries’ names in English and Spanish found them only in its word-frequency tables and its registry of persons). (d) The 1980s. For 1981–88 the library holds one volume on South America and one on the South Atlantic conflict, and none on Central America or the Caribbean; the Latin American scope has only about 400 documents dated in the 1980s, against about 3,200 for the 1970s, so Reagan-era recognition decisions cannot be answered from it. The shelf is at the Reagan Library, and the finding aids shipped with the app name it: the National Security Council’s Latin American Affairs Directorate records (NARA 12024796; series dated 1983–1989) and the White House Central Files’ subject files on South and Central America and on Argentina, Bolivia, Chile, El Salvador, Guatemala, Honduras, Nicaragua and Panama (1981–1989); that data gives their identifiers and dates but no access status. The controls for these searches were a phrase that must occur in nearly every volume (it did in 552 of 553) and an impossible phrase that must occur in none (it occurred in none).",
